  "preamble": "Reduction of the alveolar trill [r] to a single-contact tap [ɾ] turns several rapid tongue-tip contacts into one. It happened historically in Greek, where Ancient Greek ρ, described as a trill, is prototypically a tap in the modern language. In Polish, /r/ is traditionally classed as a trill but is realized as a tap in about 95% of intervocalic tokens.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Lenition, Reduction",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Greek (Ancient > Modern)",
      "languageFamily": "Hellenic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Ancient Greek ῥῆμα /r̥ɛ̂ː.ma/ with ρ = trill",
          "to": "Modern Greek ρήμα /ˈɾi.ma/ 'verb'",
          "note": "Ancient rho was an alveolar trill; the only Modern Greek rhotic is prototypically an alveolar tap, with a trill surviving mainly in clusters."
        },
        {
          "from": "Ancient Greek χείρ 'hand' (trilled ρ)",
          "to": "Modern Greek χέρι /ˈçe.ɾi/"
        }
      ]
    },
